    {
      "id": "04",
      "title": "REST or GraphQL",
      "question": "Would you expose the payment API as REST or GraphQL?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question four. Would you expose the payment API as REST, or as GraphQL? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "<em>REST</em> or <em>GraphQL</em> for a payment API?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "For the public payment API, REST wins. Each operation is a clear resource creation, like a POST to charges, which maps cleanly onto idempotency keys, caching and rate limits. GraphQL shines elsewhere: a merchant dashboard that wants sales totals, top customers and recent payments in one query. But one mutation doing several things can fail in murky ways, and arbitrary queries need cost limits.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Money over <em>REST</em>, dashboards over GraphQL",
            "left": {
              "at": "wins",
              "label": "Public payment API",
              "title": "REST",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "POST /v1/charges\nPOST /v1/charges/{id}/capture\nPOST /v1/refunds",
              "body": "One resource per operation: idempotency, caching and rate limits stay simple."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "shines",
              "label": "Merchant dashboard",
              "title": "GraphQL",
              "code": "{ sales(range: WEEK) { total }\n  topCustomers(n: 5) { name } }",
              "body": "Exactly the data needed, one round trip; needs query cost limits."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "murky",
              "text": "Multi-step mutations fail in murky ways: keep money on REST.",
              "code": "REST + GraphQL"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Every POST takes an idempotency key, so retries never double charge. Card numbers go only to the vault; everything else holds tokens. Webhooks are at least once, with backoff, so merchants dedupe by event ID. An append-only ledger of debits and credits is the source of truth. And use REST for the payment API, GraphQL for dashboards.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Payment gateway, <em>in five lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "retries",
                "text": "Idempotency keys make retries safe.",
                "code": "Idempotency-Key"
              },
              {
                "at": "vault",
                "text": "Only the vault sees card numbers.",
                "code": "tok_… everywhere else"
              },
              {
                "at": "dedupe",
                "text": "Webhooks: at least once, with backoff.",
                "code": "dedupe on event ID"
              },
              {
                "at": "ledger",
                "text": "An append-only ledger is the source of truth.",
                "code": "debits = credits"
              },
              {
                "at": "dashboards",
                "text": "REST for payments, GraphQL for dashboards.",
                "code": "POST /v1/charges"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
